{{Short description|Overview of professional wrestling in Okaiken}}
{{about|history of professional wrestling in Okaiken|wrestling style popularized in the country|Sanjin Puro}}

[[File:Rikidōzan.jpg|thumb|250px|[[Soga Ujio]], known as the "Father of Sanjin Wrestling," whose career helped define the sport during the Post-War Recovery.]]
{{Professional wrestling sidebar}}
'''Professional wrestling in [[Okaiken]]''' (commonly referred to as {{nihongo|'''Sanjin Puro'''|三人プロ}} or simply '''Puro''') is a major cultural pillar and one of the nation's most popular spectator sports. While the sport's foundations lie in the pre-war era, it became a national phenomenon during the [[Sanese Industrial Miracle]] of the 1950s. The first Sanese person to involve themselves in [[catch wrestling]] was the former [[sumo]] champion [[Tokuyama "Iron" Masaru]]. Subsequent efforts to popularize the sport remained niche until the rise of its first true megastar, [[Soga Ujio]], in 1951. Soga, often called the "Saint of the Ring," founded the '''Okaiken Wrestling Association''' (OWA) and utilized the burgeoning power of television to captivate a nation recovering from the [[Great Adonian War]].

Following Soga's death in 1963, professional wrestling branched into several major [[Professional wrestling promotion|promotions]] and [[#Styles|styles]], producing icons such as [[Honda Tadakatsu (wrestler)|Honda "The Shogun" Tadakatsu]], [[Giant Inoue]], [[Hayabusa]], and [[Tiger Mask]]. In the modern era, the sport is characterized by its "Strong Style" philosophy—a blend of martial arts realism and dramatic storytelling. Today, '''New Okaiken Pro-Wrestling''' (NOPW) is considered the premier promotion, regularly selling out the Arena Kakutou in [[Okami]], while regional strongholds in [[Yanbaru]] and [[Nakamizu]] have developed their own distinctive wrestling cultures.

== Professional wrestling style ==
{{Main|Sanjin Puro}}
Sanjin Puro is the predominant style of professional wrestling in Okaiken. Derived from the Sanese pronunciation of "Professional Wrestling," the term has become synonymous with the "Masugu" (Straight/Direct) culture of the nation. Growing out of traditional [[Lurandia|Lurandian]] catch wrestling, Sanese wrestling has evolved into a unique discipline that prioritizes [[sport psychology]], stiff physical contact, and "Fighting Spirit" (''Zettai-Tamashii'').

Unlike the high-theatric presentation often found in Western promotions, Sanese matches are presented as legitimate athletic contests. The stories told in the ring emphasize endurance, perseverance through pain, and the warrior code of the samurai class. This realism is bolstered by the mandatory inclusion of martial arts training, such as [[Judo]] and [[Karate]], for all aspiring wrestlers.

=== Regional styles ===

==== Yagikoshu Strong Style ====
The island of [[Yagikoshu]], home to the capital of Okami and the historic castle tow of [[Toyozawa]], developed the most rigid form of Sanjin Puro. Wrestlers trained in the Yagikoshu dojos emphasize forearm strikes, suplex variations, and the "fighting spirit" philosophy that requires wrestlers to no-sell damage during dramatic comebacks. The style reflects the martial heritage of the Honda Shogunate, incorporating elements from historical swordsmanship and armored combat.

Prominent Yagikoshu-trained wrestlers include [[Honda Tadakatsu (wrestler)|Honda Tadakatsu]], [[Soga Ujio]], and the legendary [[Giant Inoue]]. The Toyozawa-based [[KANRA]] dojo is considered the most prestigious training facility in the nation.

==== Harujima Lucha Libre ====
The tropical northern island of [[Harujima]], with its proximity to [[Yanbaru]] and the [[Shendan Ocean]] trade routes, developed a hybrid style incorporating high-flying techniques from [[Volisania]] and traditional maritime acrobatics. Known for its emphasis on aerial maneuvers and rapid-paced sequences, Harujima wrestlers frequently utilize the ropes and turnbuckles more than their Yagikoshu counterparts.

The style gained prominence in the 1990s through wrestlers like [[Hayabusa]] and [[Tiger Mask]], who combined Mexican lucha libre techniques with Sanese martial arts discipline. The [[Aizawa]]-based '''Dragon's Claw Dojo''' produces the majority of high-flying talent.

==== Yanbaru Island Style ====
{{Main|Yanbaru Professional Wrestling}}
The island nation of [[Yanbaru]], while politically independent, shares deep cultural ties with Okaiken's wrestling scene. Yanbaru wrestlers incorporate traditional Polynesian dancing and warrior rituals into their entrances, creating a distinctive pageantry rarely seen in mainland promotions. The style emphasizes raw power moves, headbutts, and crowd interaction, with wrestlers often performing impromptu dances to whip audiences into a frenzy.

Yanbaru wrestlers such as [[Kozo "The Spear" Togashi]] and the island legendary tag team '''The Coconut Brothers''' have achieved significant success in NOPW. The [[Yomitan]]-based '''Pacific Thunder Dojo''' serves as the primary training facility for Yanbaru wrestlers seeking to compete in mainland Okaiken.

==== Nakamian Strong-Technico ====
From [[Nakamizu]], the Sanese nation located south of Okaiken across the [[Strait of Nakami]], emerged a unique blend of technical wrestling and brawling. Known as "Strong-Technico," this style prioritizes submission holds, chain wrestling sequences, and technical counter-wrestling while maintaining the physical stiffness of Yagikoshu style. Nakamian wrestlers often have backgrounds in collegian wrestling and amateur wrestling circuits.

Notable Nakamian wrestlers include [[Kawada Masahiro]] and [[Takagi Yuto]], both of whom have held the [[IGO World Heavyweight Championship]]. The [[Miyagami]]-based '''Southern Strong Dojo''' exports talent throughout the Sanese archipelago.

== Professional wrestling on television ==
Since its inception, Sanjin Puro has been intrinsically linked to the growth of Sanese [[television]]. In the 1950s, Soga Ujio's matches were broadcast by '''Okami TV''', often attracting massive crowds to public viewing screens in the capital. As the sport grew, major networks like '''Toyozawa Broadcasting''' and '''Ishijima Air''' competed for broadcast rights, eventually splitting the talent pool between the top two students of Soga: [[Giant Inoue]] and [[Honda Tadakatsu (wrestler)|Honda Tadakatsu]].

During the [[Quiet Revolution]] of 1968, wrestling programming was briefly used as a tool for national unity, though the industry faced challenges during the economic volatility of the late 1970s. The advent of [[cable television]] in the 1980s allowed for the rise of "Shoot-Style" promotions like '''KAZAN''', which leaned into the MMA-lite presentation. Since 2014, NOPW has utilized the '''Satori Sky-Sync''' satellite network to broadcast live specials to international audiences, particularly in [[Andalus]] and [[Balisca]].

