== Major promotions ==

=== New Okaiken Pro-Wrestling (NOPW) ===
{{Main|New Okaiken Pro-Wrestling}}
Founded in 1972 by former OWA wrestler [[Antonio Hama]], '''New Okaiken Pro-Wrestling''' (NOPW) has grown to become the largest and most prestigious wrestling promotion in Okaiken. Headquartered in [[Okami]], NOPW annually produces over 200 live events and maintains exclusive contracts with over 100 wrestlers.

NOPW's roster is divided into several distinct factions, including the prestigious '''CHAOS''' stable, the villainous '''Cannon Club''', and the traditional '''Seigigun''' (Regular Army). The promotion's top championship, the [[IGO Heavyweight Championship]], is considered the most prestigious title in Sanese professional wrestling.

The promotion operates several "brands" under its umbrella:
* '''NOPW World''' - The flagship brand featuring heavyweights and main event talent
* '''NOPW STRONG''' - Featuring junior heavyweights and international talent
* '''NOPW TAMASHII''' - Focuses on hardcore and deathmatch-style wrestling

=== All Okaiken Pro Wrestling (AOPW) ===
{{Main|All Okaiken Pro Wrestling}}
Formed in 2000 by former NOPW wrestlers including [[Honda Tadakatsu]]'s son [[Honda Masanori]], AOPW positions itself as a more athlete-focused alternative to the entertainment-heavy NOPW. The promotion maintains strong ties to mixed martial arts and frequently cross-promotes events with MMA organizations.

AOPW's signature style emphasizes technical wrestling and legitimate combat sports credentials. Notable champions include former Olympic judoka [[Yamamoto Kosei]] and sambo champion [[Viktor Dragov]] from [[Asmyria]].

=== Pro Wrestling MIGHTY ===
Based in [[Aizawa]] on Harujima, MIGHTY has cultivated a reputation for producing innovative junior heavyweight wrestling. The promotion consistently exports talent to larger organizations and has served as a springboard for international careers.

Notable graduates include [[Ryohei "Speed Star" Maeda]], who achieved significant success in [[Lurandia]] wrestling circuits, and current roster member [[KAZUKI]], a high-flying sensation who regularly headlines MIGHTY events.

=== KAZAN Fight Spirit ===
The premier "shoot-style" promotion, KAZAN blends professional wrestling with legitimate fighting techniques. Based in [[Seto]], the promotion attracts former MMA fighters and wrestlers seeking a more realistic presentation.

== Notable wrestlers ==
=== Native Okaiken stars ===

==== Honda Tadakatsu ====
{{Main|Honda Tadakatsu (wrestler)}}
Born [[Tokuyama Tetsuya]] in [[Yonezawa Prefecture]], Honda Tadakatsu ({{nihongo|本多忠勝|Honda Tadakatsu}}) adopted his ring name in homage to the legendary Honda Shogunate general. Standing 183cm and weighing 115kg, Honda's imposing presence and devastating lariat made him the defining star of 1980s Okaiken wrestling. His feud with [[Giant Inoue]] in 1984-1986 remains one of the highest-grossing programs in OWA history, with their [[Toyozawa Dome]] match drawing 52,000 fans.

Honda's signature moves include the '''Shogun Lariat''', a running clothesline performed with such force it became synonymous with instant knockouts, and the '''Three Star Press''', a top-rope diving attack. He retired in 1998 but continues appears regularly as a special referee and ambassador for NOPW.

==== Hayabusa ====
{{Main|Hayabusa (wrestler)}}
Born [[Fuyuki Toshihiro]] in [[Mito]], Hayabusa revolutionized Harujima-style wrestling with his innovative aerial techniques. His [[Phoenix Splash]]—a 450° corkscrew splash—became one of the most emulated moves in modern wrestling. Tragically, his career was cut short in 2001 when a botched moorunner resulted in a severe spinal injury.

Despite never fully recovering mobility, Hayabusa remains a beloved figure, mentoring younger wrestlers at his '''Falcon's Nest Dojo''' in [[Mito]]. His influence on high-flying wrestling across Adonia is immeasurable.

==== Tiger Mask ====
{{Main|Tiger Mask}}
The Tiger Mask persona represents one of wrestling's most enduring legacies. Created in 1982 as a way to capitalize on the popular anime character, the mask has been passed down through five generations of wrestlers. Each Tiger Mask has embodied different aspects of the character:
* '''First Tiger Mask (1982-1983)''': The original, a former amateur wrestling champion who brought legitimacy to the gimmick
* '''Second Tiger Mask (1983-1991)''': Expanded the character's high-flying repertoire
* '''Third Tiger Mask (1994-1998)''': Added mixed martial arts elements
* '''Fourth Tiger Mask (2002-2013)''': The longest-tenured, popularized the character internationally
* '''Fifth Tiger Mask (2016-present)''': Current holder, combines classic elements with modern athleticism

=== International stars in Okaiken ===

==== Vaiahi wrestling family ====
The [[Vaiahi]] Islands, located NNW of Okaiken in the [[Shendan Ocean]], have produced a remarkable wrestling dynasty. The [[Fifita]] family, known in Okaiken by their Sanese ring names, has dominated tag team competition for over three decades.

===== Kengo Vaiahi =====
Born {{nihongo|Tokugawa "Kengo" Tama|徳川タマ健吾}} in the Fa'amotu Islands of Vaiahi, Kengo ({{nihongo|健剛|Kengo}}, meaning "Strong Sword") rose to prominence as a founding member of the '''Cannon Club''' faction in NOPW. His background in traditional island wrestling combined with Sanese strong style training created a unique hybrid approach.

Kengo's signature '''Gun Stun''', a modified neckbreaker, and his '''Tama Twist''', a spinning cutter, have become defining moves of his career. He has held the [[IGO Tag Team Championship]] alongside his brother multiple times.

===== Malo Vaiahi =====
The younger brother of Kengo, {{nihongo|Malo Vaiahi|マロ・ヴァイアヒ}} (ring name) previously competed in [[Lurandia]] wrestling circuits before joining NOPW in 2016. Known for his technical proficiency and tag team prowess, Malo complements his brother's power game with speed and aerial awareness.

The brothers' signature '''Magic Killer''', a combination spinebuster and neckbreaker, is considered one of the most devastating tag team finishers in modern wrestling.

===== Taumafa Vaiahi =====
{{nihongo|Taumafa Vaiahi|タウマファ・ヴァイアヒ}}, the youngest of the Fifita brothers, stands an imposing 198cm and weighs 145kg. His power-based offense draws comparisons to [[Giant Inoue]] in his prime. Taumafa held the [[Strong Openweight Championship]] twice, establishing himself as a legitimate main event threat.

==== The Reigning Dynasty ====

===== Leoni Masua =====
{{Main|Leoni Masua}}
Born {{nihongo|Masua Leoni|マスア・レオニ}} in {{nihongo|Fangatapu|ファンガタプ}} in Vaiahi, Leoni represents the pinnacle of Vaiahi wrestling royalty. The son of legendary island grappler {{nihongo|Sika Masua|シカ・マスア}}, Leoni was trained in the traditional warrior arts of his homeland before joining his cousins in the Fifita wrestling dynasty.

Standing 191cm and weighing 120kg, Leoni's '''Spear of Fangatapu''', a devastating running spear, is considered one of the most impactful finishing moves in professional wrestling. His nearly three-year reign as [[IGO Heavyweight Champion]] (2022-2025) stands as one of the longest in NOPW history.

Leoni's presentation combines Vaiahi cultural elements with the pageantry of main event wrestling. His entrances feature traditional island warrior dancing and the carrying of the '''Namo''', a ceremonial war club, signifying his status as a {{nihongo|Tribal Chief|部族の長}} within storyline mythology.

===== The Usos =====
The tag team of {{nihongo|Tama Uso|タマ・ウソ}} and {{nihongo|Riku Uso|リク・ウソ}}, twin brothers also from the Vaiahi wrestling dynasty, have dominated NOPW tag team competition. Their {{nihongo|Island Uppercut|アイランド・アッパーカット}} and {{nihongo|Double Splash|ダブル・スプラッシュ}} have made them [[IGO Tag Team Champions]] seven times.

